  <h1>India’s Judicial Pendency — Causes, Impacts, and Reforms</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India’s judiciary is often hailed as the guardian of constitutional rights. Yet, as The Hindu reported in 2024, judicial pendency has reached unprecedented levels, undermining both justice delivery and public trust. For UPSC, this issue is central to polity, governance, and social justice.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Total Pending Cases (2024): ~5 crore (SC: 80,000+, HCs: 60 lakh+, District Courts: ~4.3 crore).<br/>•	Case Clearance Rate: ~60–70% in most courts.<br/>•	Judges-to-Population Ratio: ~21 per million (recommended: 50).<br/>•	Vacancies: 30% in HCs, 20% in lower courts.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Causes<br/>1.	Vacant Judicial Posts: Delayed appointments, collegium–executive tussles.<br/>2.	Procedural Delays: Adjournments, long trials, outdated practices.<br/>3.	Infrastructure Deficit: Many courts lack staff, IT systems.<br/>4.	Rising Litigation: Increasing PILs, commercial disputes, government as largest litigant.<br/>5.	Limited Technology Use: E-courts underutilised.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Impacts<br/>•	Justice Delayed = Justice Denied: Undermines faith in judiciary.<br/>•	Economic Costs: Contract enforcement slow, hurting investment climate.<br/>•	Human Rights: Undertrials languish in jails.<br/>•	Social Inequality: Poor disproportionately affected.<br/>•	Governance Burden: Courts intervene in executive policy due to backlogs.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government & Judicial Measures<br/>•	E-Courts Mission Mode Project: Case digitisation, virtual hearings.<br/>•	Fast-Track & Special Courts: For POCSO, commercial disputes.<br/>•	National Judicial Data Grid (NJDG): Case tracking and transparency.<br/>•	All India Judicial Service (Proposed): Centralised recruitment.<br/>•	Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR): Lok Adalats, mediation.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>•	Resistance to Change: Traditional legal culture.<br/>•	Coordination Issues: Centre–state responsibility overlaps.<br/>•	Insufficient ADR Uptake: Mediation/conciliation underused.<br/>•	Quality vs Quantity: Increasing judges without training may affect justice quality.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Rule of Law: Delays weaken democracy.<br/>•	Economic Development: Strong judiciary improves ease of doing business.<br/>•	Social Justice: Vulnerable groups need speedy redress.<br/>•	International Image: Judicial efficiency key to investor confidence.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Polity & Governance): Judiciary, reforms, separation of powers.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Justice delayed is justice denied.”<br/>o	“Judicial reforms are the key to democratic deepening.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Institutions and governance.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Fill Vacancies: Fast-track appointments and training.<br/>2.	Digital Push: Expand e-courts, AI-based case management.<br/>3.	ADR & Mediation: Make pre-litigation mediation mandatory in civil cases.<br/>4.	Infrastructure: Modern courtrooms, staff, IT systems.<br/>5.	Government Role: Reduce frivolous litigation (responsible litigation policy).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Judicial pendency is not just a legal issue but a governance and development challenge. Addressing it requires institutional reform, technology adoption, and cultural change within the legal system. For UPSC aspirants, it represents the intersection of polity, governance, and social justice.</p>
